---
/**
 * /cookie-policy — what this site stores in the visitor's browser.
 *
 * Kept deliberately specific rather than boilerplate: the named keys below are
 * the ones actually set in BaseLayout.astro (dait_sess / dait_visitor). The
 * Google and Meta entries depend on siteConfig.analytics + allowIndexing. If
 * any of those change, update this page and /privacy-policy together.
 */
import LegalLayout from "../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { siteConfig } from "../config/site";
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Cookie Policy"
  description="What DAIT Institute stores in your browser, why, and how to control or clear it — including analytics cookies and our own first-party attribution keys."
  canonicalPath="/cookie-policy/"
  updated="2026-09-23"
  intro="A plain-English list of what this website stores in your browser, what each item is for, and how to remove it."
>
  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <p>
    We don't use cookies to build advertising profiles of you, and we don't sell anything about your
    browsing. What we store falls into two groups: a couple of small items we set ourselves to
    understand how people find us, and analytics from Google.
  </p>

  <h2>What we set ourselves</h2>
  <p>
    These are stored using your browser's own storage rather than traditional cookies. They stay on
    your device and are only sent to us if you choose to submit an enquiry form — they help us
    understand which pages and campaigns lead to enquiries.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><code>dait_sess</code></strong> (session storage) — the page you landed on, how many
      pages you've viewed in this visit, where you were referred from, and which visit number this
      is. <strong>Cleared automatically when you close the tab.</strong>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>dait_visitor</code></strong> (local storage) — the date you first visited and a
      count of how many times you've visited. <strong>Stays until you clear your browser data.</strong>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Neither contains your name, phone number, email address or any other detail that identifies you
    personally.
  </p>

  <h2>Analytics</h2>
  <p>
    We use <strong>Google Analytics</strong>, loaded through Google Tag Manager, to see how many
    people visit, which pages they read and which programs get the most interest. This sets
    Google's own cookies, typically:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>_ga</code></strong> and <strong><code>_ga_&lt;id&gt;</code></strong> — distinguish one visitor from another (up to 2 years)</li>
    <li><strong><code>_gid</code></strong> — distinguishes visitors over a short period (24 hours)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This tells us patterns, not identities. Google's handling of this data is governed by its own
    privacy terms, and you can opt out of Google Analytics across all websites using
    <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google's browser add-on</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Advertising</h2>
  <p>
    When we are running campaigns on Facebook or Instagram, a <strong>Meta advertising pixel</strong>
    may be active on this site and set cookies such as <code>_fbp</code>. These let us measure
    whether an ad led to an enquiry, and show relevant ads to people who have visited us. If we are
    not running campaigns, this is switched off entirely.
  </p>

  <h2>WhatsApp</h2>
  <p>
    Our WhatsApp button is a normal link — it opens WhatsApp and sets nothing on this site. Once you
    are in WhatsApp, Meta's own terms apply to that conversation.
  </p>

  <h2>Your choice</h2>
  <p>
    The first time you visit, we ask before loading anything from Google or Meta. Choose
    <strong>Essential only</strong> and none of it ever loads. You can change your mind at any
    time:
  </p>
  <p>
    <button
      type="button"
      id="reset-cookie-choice"
      class="inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-lg border-2 border-accent-500 px-5 py-2 text-sm font-bold text-accent-600 transition-colors hover:bg-accent-50 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent-600"
    >
      Change my cookie choice
    </button>
    <span id="reset-cookie-note" class="ml-3 text-sm text-ink-500" hidden>Choice cleared — reloading…</span>
  </p>

  <h2>How to control this</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Clear our first-party items</strong> — clearing site data for this website in your
      browser settings removes <code>dait_sess</code> and <code>dait_visitor</code> immediately.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Block cookies</strong> — every major browser lets you block or delete cookies, either
      for all sites or just this one. Look under Settings → Privacy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Opt out of Google Analytics</strong> — use the browser add-on linked above.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Browse privately</strong> — a private or incognito window discards all of this when
      you close it.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Blocking any of the above will not stop you using this website. Every page, program detail and
    enquiry form works exactly the same without them.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If we add or remove anything that stores data in your browser, we will update this page and the
    "last updated" date above. For the wider picture of what we do with enquiry information, see our
    <a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
  </p>
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var btn = document.getElementById("reset-cookie-choice");
    var note = document.getElementById("reset-cookie-note");
    if (!btn) return;
    btn.addEventListener("click", function () {
      try { localStorage.removeItem("dait_consent"); } catch (e) {}
      if (note) note.hidden = false;
      setTimeout(function () { location.reload(); }, 600);
    });
  })();
</script>

</LegalLayout>
